## 3. The correct mental model

Wrong model:

```text
User login lives inside a worker.
Worker stops.
Login dies.
User logs in again tomorrow.
```

Correct model:

```text
User workspace owns channel connection metadata.
Gateway owns the live channel runtime.
Session state is persisted by the selected placement policy.
Heavy worker wakes only when needed.
Control plane owns policy, audit, approval, billing, registry, revocation.
```

Lifecycle:

```text
User connects Telegram once.
Gateway creates Telegram session state.
Gateway stores session state in its gateway state directory.
Gateway listens or reconnects.
A heavy Sage/agent worker is started only when needed.
Worker uses gateway to act.
Worker stops.
Gateway and/or session state survives.
```

The rule:

```text
Agent worker can die.
Gateway should not die during always-on mode.
Gateway can die.
Login/session should not die if persisted correctly.
VPS/container can die.
Live answering stops until another gateway starts, but login/session can survive only if state exists outside the destroyed runtime or volume survives.
No running gateway = no real-time answering.
Persistent session is not the same thing as live presence.
```

