## 8. Security threat model

Assume attackers exist. Because they do. Humans built the internet and then acted surprised.

### 8.1 Attackers

Threat actors:

```text
external network attacker
malicious user
malicious workspace member
compromised hosted VPS
compromised user-provided VPS
stolen gateway token
stolen session token
stolen personal channel session file
prompt injection through Telegram/WhatsApp messages
provider webhook spoofing
supply-chain attack
malicious marketplace package
insider/admin abuse
cloud database leak
misconfigured dev/prod environment
```

### 8.2 Security objective

The goal is not "impossible to hack." That phrase belongs in marketing trash fires.

The real goal:

```text
reduce attack probability
reduce blast radius
make abuse visible
fail closed
allow fast revocation
prevent cross-tenant leakage
protect personal channel session material
avoid uncontrolled outbound actions
```

### 8.3 Absolute rules

```text
Never store personal channel session files in public Studio connector state.
Never send raw tokens/cookies/session strings to frontend.
Never put raw secrets in heartbeat metadata.
Never put raw secrets in logs, diagnostics, activity, approval cards, or traces.
Never give user-owned VPS platform master secrets.
Never share one browser profile/session dir across users.
Never allow two active gateways to own the same personal channel session.
Never allow a deployed Studio agent to inherit Sage Full Access by default.
Never allow production auth-disabled mode.
Never continue if policy/placement/session state is unclear.
```

### 8.4 Security controls already aligned in docs/code

Repo documents already define:

```text
tenant/workspace isolation
BYOK secrets through broker boundaries
fail-closed policy
strict API gateways
UI stays dumb
activity ledger
audit trails
gateway selected/registered checks
workspace match checks
device trust and revocation
fresh heartbeat requirement
capability readiness checks
risk classification
approval gates
kill switch
safe mode
secret redaction
```

These are not optional. They are the product.

### 8.5 Hosted VPS security baseline

For company-provided hosted gateway containers:

```text
one workspace per gateway/container
one persistent volume per gateway
no SSH access for customer
non-root runtime where possible
read-only filesystem except state/log dirs
restricted outbound network where possible
no cloud admin credentials inside container
gateway token scoped to one gateway/workspace
short-lived WebSocket session token
rotation and revocation path
separate container logs per gateway
logs redacted before display
resource limits: CPU/RAM/disk
idle shutdown for heavy workers
always-on only for lightweight gateway
```

### 8.6 User-provided VPS security baseline

For self-hosted/user-provided VPS:

```text
assume user/root can inspect local files
keep only scoped gateway credentials on it
never put platform-wide secrets there
warn user that VPS owner controls that machine
support revoke/re-pair
workspace match must be enforced
host fingerprint verification should be strongly recommended or required for serious setup
```

Important:

```text
If the user provides the VPS, you cannot make the runtime invisible to them.
You can make Empyralis cloud safe from that VPS.
You cannot hide files from root forever.
```

### 8.7 Company-provided VPS user visibility

If Empyralis provides the VPS, the user should not see the machine.

User sees:

```text
Agent Computer name
region
status
connected channels
session health
last heartbeat
activity/audit
cost/usage
start/stop/pause/revoke
logs after redaction
```

User does not see:

```text
SSH terminal
raw filesystem
raw cookies
raw Telegram session string
raw WhatsApp auth state
container image internals
system logs with secrets
```

### 8.8 Secret storage and encryption

MVP `gateway_local` mode:

```text
state files under persistent volume
0600 file permissions where possible
provider/VPS volume encryption if available
no raw secret display
no raw secret logs
```

Future encrypted backup/vault mode:

```text
envelope encryption
per-workspace data encryption key
key reference separate from blob
encrypted object storage blob
versioned session state
integrity hash
recovery audit event
deletion/export flow
key rotation
BYOK option for enterprise
```

### 8.9 Gateway lease/fencing requirement

Before serious always-on/failover, add cloud-side lease/fencing.

Reason:

```text
A local gateway.lock protects one state directory.
It does not prevent two different machines from trying to own the same personal channel session.
```

Required table:

```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS personal_channel_leases (
  lease_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  tenant_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  workspace_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  user_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  channel_connection_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  channel_key TEXT NOT NULL,
  gateway_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  device_id TEXT,
  placement_kind TEXT NOT NULL,
  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active',
  acquired_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
  expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  heartbeat_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
  fencing_token BIGINT NOT NULL,
  release_reason TEXT,
  metadata JSONB NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'::jsonb,
  UNIQUE (workspace_id, channel_connection_id)
);
```

Every outbound send must include:

```text
channel_connection_id
gateway_id
lease_id
fencing_token
idempotency_key
```

Backend rejects stale lease/fencing tokens.

### 8.10 Outbound idempotency

Do not send duplicate replies after reconnect/retry.

Add/verify table:

```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS personal_channel_outbound_dedup (
  idempotency_key TEXT NOT NULL,
  workspace_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  channel_connection_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  remote_jid TEXT NOT NULL,
  gateway_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  external_message_id TEXT,
  status TEXT NOT NULL,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
  PRIMARY KEY (workspace_id, channel_connection_id, idempotency_key)
);
```

